@@ -33,7 +33,7 @@
 <p>Then the search itself, and the sentence that gives this essay its title. Radio contact "requires some serious material investment, as it turns out, if it is to be actualized… This kind of investment would have to be even more substantial than the current investment in research on nuclear energy" (p. 90). And he knows where the obstacle sits, which is not in the sky: "No doubt scientists still need to 'cultivate' a generation of rulers who will be inclined to dip deeply enough into the public coffers to finance issues that, worrying as it may seem, traditionally belong to science fiction territory" (p. 90).</p>
 <p>Inside the same paragraph he identifies the asymmetry that has since become the field's central political problem. Listening is cheap relative to shouting, so everyone will listen: "If, for economic reasons, all the civilizations were only working on reception, then no one would ever hear anyone else, as many have already pointed out" (p. 90).</p>
 <p>And then the information argument, which is the best thing in the chapter. The better a message is encoded, the less it looks like a message: "the more precisely the transmitted message uses the capacity of the information channel (i.e., the higher the reduction of the excess of this transmission), the more it starts to resemble noise" (p. 90). The consequence is that a receiver who does not know the code has "tremendous difficulties not just with deciphering the information he receives but also with recognizing it precisely as information, as opposed to cosmic background noise" (p. 90). So — "We cannot thus exclude the possibility that already today, we are receiving, as noise, via our radio telescopes, fragments of 'interstellar conversations'" (p. 90). It follows that anyone who wants to be found must deliberately waste bandwidth, sending "special 'call signs' with a relatively simple, clearly ordered, and repeatable structure" (p. 90), which are a fraction of their traffic — which makes the receiving equipment, once again, the expensive part.</p>
-<p>The mid-1960s baseline: Shklovsky's monograph of 1962; Cocconi and Morrison's proposal and Drake's Ozma; the Byurakan conference of 1964 where Kardashev proposed his three energy types; and no exoplanet detected anywhere, by anyone. <!-- CHECK: baseline-1964-ch3 --></p>
+<p>The baseline: Shklovsky's monograph of 1962; Cocconi and Morrison's proposal and Drake's Ozma; the Byurakan conference of 1964 where Kardashev proposed his three energy types; and no exoplanet detected anywhere, by anyone — bearing in mind that this translation follows the altered fourth edition of 1974, so the text is mid-1960s as revised. <!-- CHECK: baseline-1964-ch3 --></p>
