+# Review: Essay 3 — `chapters/03-who-pays-for-the-telescope.html`
+
+Findings only. Ranked by severity.
+
+---
+
+### 1. The cost MISS prices the wrong thing, then the essay uses that cheapness to unsay the MISS
+
+**Location:** ledger object row, “This kind of investment would have to be even more substantial than the current investment in research on nuclear energy” → **MISS**; “What happened”: “A serious search never cost anything like nuclear-energy research and never needed to”; “Where he was worst”: “By his own condition, we are not yet entitled to the conclusion the chapter reaches for. The silence may be… what $10 million a year sounds like.”
+
+**Problem:** On p. 90 the investment is the cost of *actualizing* contact: “construct a great amount of equipment that would enable a ‘search for extraterrestrials’—and possibly also transmitting stations,” restated as “constructing a sizeable amount of specialized receiving eq# Review: Essay 3 — `chapters/03-who-pays-for-the-telescope.html`
+
+Findings only. Ranked by severity.
+
+---
+
+### 1. The cost MISS prices the wrong thing, then the essay uses that cheapness to unsay the MISS
+
+**Location:** ledger object row, “This kind of investment would have to be even more substantial than the current investment in research on nuclear energy” → **MISS**; “What happened”: “A serious search never cost anything like nuclear-energy research and never needed to”; “Where he was worst”: “By his own condition, we are not yet entitled to the conclusion the chapter reaches for. The silence may be… what $10 million a year sounds like.”
+
+**Problem:** On p. 90 the investment is the cost of *actualizing* contact: “construct a great amount of equipment that would enable a ‘search for extraterrestrials’—and possibly also transmitting stations,” restated as “constructing a sizeable amount of specialized receiving equipment… an extremely costly one.” The 2026 cell pays this with HRMS / Breakthrough Listen *program* spend (~$10M/year). Those programs rent time on Green Bank, Parkes, MeerKAT and (via partnership) FAST — dishes built with public capital at hundreds of millions to billions. That is not the equipment bill he named.
+
+The same essay then says the $10M search is too small to meet his p. 80 condition, so the null result cannot be scored. Those two verdicts cannot both be earned by the same figure. If $10M/year *is* the actualized search, the p. 80 row is not OPEN. If it is *not* the actualized search, the cost claim has not been tested and cannot be a MISS. “Never needed to” is the clause that is doing the work, and it is an inference, not a receipt.
+
+**Fix:** Score the nuclear-comparison sentence only against the quantity he named (dedicated search-and-transmit capital, not an observing grant). If the complete search has not been built, the row is **OPEN** (or **EARLY** if SKA-class public arrays are the live path). If the claim is only that *society never spent* nuclear-research money on SETI, say that and do not also call $10M/year a serious search that “never needed” more. Do not let one number pay a MISS and an OPEN that deny each other.
+
+---
+
+### 2. “Every serious attempt to leave or to listen has been paid for by individuals” is false
+
+**Location:** “What happened,” last paragraph: “Every serious attempt to leave or to listen has been paid for by individuals”; same claim in weaker form throughout: “The answer was none of them”; “the rulers never came.” Only `starshot-2016` marks the paragraph.
+
+**Problem:** Ozma was NRAO. HRMS was NASA. Breakthrough Listen buys time on NSF/CSIRO/SARAO instruments and partners with China’s FAST, a state-built 500-metre dish that does SETI. NASA reopened technosignature proposals after a 2018 congressional direction. The ATA itself ran on NSF money before the 2011 collapse. The sentence as written is not a compression; it is wrong. It is also unmarked: `starshot-2016` does not cover listening.
+
+A skeptic who knows FAST or Green Bank exists will stop at this sentence. The political story (US dedicated radio SETI is privately topped up) can be told without the universal.
+
+**Fix:** Bound the claim to US *dedicated program* money after 1993, and say the dishes are public. Mention FAST (and SKA as a SETI science case) or drop “every” / “none of them.” Give the stronger sentence its own `<!-- CHECK -->` or cut it.
+
+---
+
+### 3. The “rulers” sentence is not a forecast; the ledger then scores the same pages twice
+
+**Location:** “What happened”: “His political forecast, though, was exact”; object row “cultivate a generation of rulers who will be inclined to dip deeply enough into the public coffers” → **RIGHT QUESTION, WRONG ANSWER**; problem row “Contact is a problem of cost and incentive… (pp. 89–90)” → **HIT**.
+
+**Problem:** Corpus p. 90 is hortatory: “No doubt scientists still need to ‘cultivate’ a generation of rulers…” He states a present need, not that the cultivation will succeed. RQWA treats non-arrival of those rulers as a failed prediction. The prose (“was exact”) then scores the same sentence as a HIT.
+
+The object-block RQWA is a problem-level verdict on a political mechanism. The next row HITs a thematic paraphrase of pp. 89–90 (“cost and incentive”) paid by the same funding history. CONTEXT §4 wants one specific claim against one specific fact; this is the essay’s thesis counted twice, once as RQWA and once as HIT.
+
+**Fix:** Drop the rulers row, or score the hortation as what it is (a diagnosis of where the bottleneck sits) at problem level, once. Cut “was exact.” Do not HIT a summary of the two pages after already grading their parts.
+
+---
+
+### 4. The chapter’s load-bearing miss is sitting in the “said” section, unscored
+
+**Location:** “Bracewell and von Hoerner worked ‘from the premise that only 1 out of 150 stars in our Galaxy has planets’ (p. 63)”; Reading: “‘A Metatheory of Miracles’… belongs to a different essay”; CONTEXT §6 job for this essay: “von Hoerner/Drake-era statistics, his ‘catastrophic theory’, votum separatum. Test against exoplanet census, SETI negatives, the modern Fermi literature.”
+
+**Problem:** He called 1-in-150 “a rather modest estimate” (p. 63). Planet occurrence is of order one per star. That inverts the input to the silence puzzle the chapter is built on. The essay quotes the number and never grades it. Essay 2 was blocked for the same move: a chosen pitch does not amend §6. CONTEXT’s 2026-08-18 note that 3-D leaves the famous material “to a second pass” does not repeal the contract.
+
+If the cost MISS in (1) is withdrawn, this essay’s only negative is gone, and the honest one is here. Even if 3-D’s scope is kept, leaving the inverted premise as scenery is how “Lem predicted everything” survives a chapter whose statistics broke.
+
+**Fix:** Add an object row on the 1-in-150 premise (reported, then endorsed as modest) against the exoplanet census. Either expand to the §6 job (hypothesis III, votum, the silence-as-data question) or amend §6 in CONTEXT before shipping a two-page excerpt as essay 3.
+
+---
+
+### 5. Note 3 and the 1974 update are unread
+
+**Location:** whole essay; “Future Prospects” treated as 1964; “three sentences in a 1966 book”; no later-Lem.
+
+**Problem:** AGENT: read the chapter endnotes; where they disagree with the body, the note is usually the better forecast. Note 3 (pp. 358–359) is Lem grading the chapter: isolation of civilizations so their activity “cannot be easily observed on an astronomical scale” is “the most probable” and “the dominant hypothesis in the course of writing this book.” That is the problem-level claim of these pages, and it is closer to the METI-trap / camouflage argument than the nuclear-price sentence.
+
+Note 5 (p. 360) records that he added “And also in 1974” to “In 1966, we still do not know anything specific.” The text being graded is the 1974 edition of a 1966 insert, and he doubled down on ignorance after another decade of silence. The essay never says so.
+
+**Fix:** Quote note 3 and score isolation/camouflage as his selected hypothesis. Date Future Prospects as 1966/1974. Score 2026 against the later sentence, not only the 1964 frame.
+
+---
+
+### 6. Star-flight HIT is sixty years into a several-hundred-year negative, and it is von Hoerner’s
+
+**Location:** object row: near-light travel “likely to remain impossible over the next several hundred years” → **HIT**, paid by Starshot’s gram-scale sails.
+
+**Problem:** p. 89 reports “balance analyses of energy (such as the one provided by von Hoerner).” HIT needs the described thing to have happened. A multi-century impossibility, sixty years on, has not happened; the path is whatever it was. Starshot does not test Moon-mass annihilation; it is a different, uncrewed architecture, and the $100M was an announcement. AGENT: a demo/proposal receipts a proposal.
+
+**Fix:** **EARLY** or **OPEN**. Credit von Hoerner in the Lem cell. Receipt Starshot as a privately announced alternative, not as confirmation of the energy budget.
+
+---
+
+### 7. Reception HIT credits a cited commonplace, then erases Arecibo 1974
+
+**Location:** object row: “If, for economic reasons, all the civilizations were only working on reception…” → **HIT**; “What happened”: “a handful sent by one Russian astronomer from a dish in Crimea, and a small organisation founded in 2015”; `meti-2015` row: “the main transmissions to date were a handful sent by Alexander Zaitsev.”
+
+**Problem:** He flags the trap as already known (“as many have already pointed out”). Scoring Earth’s later METI fight as an object-level HIT is a logical conditional credited with a specific terrestrial outcome. The 2015 statement is a real instance of the mechanism; that belongs at problem level, and **HIT** is still high for a coordination problem that was live in 1964.
+
+Separately, the receipt is wrong. The Arecibo message (16 November 1974, Drake/Sagan, a US national observatory) is the most famous deliberate transmission. Cosmic Call was not Zaitsev alone. `meti-2015` states a false “main transmissions” claim.
+
+**Fix:** Move the row to problem level; quote the “as many have already pointed out” clause. Correct the transmission history (Arecibo 1974 first, then Evpatoria, then METI International). Do not let the 2015 statement pay “almost nobody transmits” as if the 1974 message did not happen.
+
+---
+
+### 8. The p. 80 condition is duration and sensitivity, not budget; the chapter already said it was too early
+
+**Location:** problem row on p. 80 → **OPEN**; “Where he was worst”: “Thirty pages treat the silence as data… By his own condition, we are not yet entitled to the conclusion the chapter reaches for.”
+
+**Problem:** The scored sentence names time and instrument sensitivity. Both increased: sixty years, and backends on GBT/FAST/MeerKAT many orders deeper than Ozma’s 27-metre dish (which the chapter itself describes on p. 64). What remains unquantified is *coverage*, which Still open #1 already states correctly. The ledger pays the OPEN with “a field running at about $10 million a year.” That substitutes a dollar figure for the variables he named.
+
+On the same page: “Today it is still too early for this.” He accepts the vacuum as material to interpret and refuses a revision of biogenesis. “Thirty pages treat the silence as data” and “the conclusion the chapter reaches for” overstate his confidence. The turn-Lem-against-himself move needs the sentence he actually wrote next.
+
+**Fix:** Receipt the OPEN with a completeness/haystack paper, not with $10M/year. Restore “Today it is still too early.” If the chapter’s conclusion is hypothesis III / isolation (note 3), say so; do not invent a stronger conclusion to unsay.
+
+---
+
+### 9. Call-signs HIT grades a method the chapter already says Drake was using
+
+**Location:** object row, efficiently coded message “starts to resemble noise” / “special ‘call signs’…” → **HIT**; “The information argument is now simply how the searches are built.”
+
+**Problem:** The Shannon point (efficient code ≈ noise, so a beacon must waste bandwidth) is his, and it held. The 2026 cell then identifies that point with “narrowband, repetitive beacons,” which is how Ozma was already designed. p. 64: equipment “specially constructed for receiving ‘artificial’ signals, thus enabling their differentiation from ‘galaxy noise’,” searching for “some kind of regularity.” “Now simply how the searches are built” reads as a forecast fulfilled. It is a correct analysis that did not change practice.
+
+The stronger claim — conversations “already today” in the archives — is hedged (“We cannot thus exclude”) and limited to “supercivilizations,” which the essay’s quote drops. Still open #3 handles that claim; the HIT should not swallow it.
+
+**Fix:** Narrow the HIT to the encoding-looks-like-noise argument, restore “supercivilizations,” and stop saying this is why searches are built the way they already were in 1960. Or grade the method **HIT** as description, not as prediction.
+
+---
+
+### 10. Unmarked or overstated 2026 sentences a reader will check
+
+**Location / problem / fix:**
+
+- “Listening to the whole sky turns out to be two orders of magnitude cheaper than not-quite-building a reactor.” Breakthrough Listen is a targeted million-star survey, not the whole sky. HRMS had a sky-survey element; Listen is not it. Mark and narrow, or cut “whole sky.”
+- “everyone has decided listening is the responsible posture.” A few dozen signatories of a 2015 statement decided that. Most of Earth decided nothing. Bound to the METI literature.
+- ATA: “came back that December partly by renting itself to the US Air Force to track orbital debris, topped up by about $200,000 from a public appeal.” Berkeley News (2012-04-13): revived December 2011 “thanks in part to several hundred thousand dollars donated by private individuals”; SRI/Air Force management is April 2012. The December restart was the appeal; the Air Force lease is later. Uncompress the dates.
+- Fusion $790M FY2025 and ITER “passed $20 billion” are close enough (FES including ITER; ITER official estimates sit around €18–22bn, with higher DOE figures). The cheat is comparing a year of SETI ops to ITER’s multi-decade construction as if they were the same kind of number — fold into finding 1, do not leave the comparison looking like annual vs annual.
+
+---
+
+### 11. Essay 3 has no rows in `checks/quotes.tsv`
+
+**Location:** `grep ^3` on `checks/quotes.tsv` is empty. The HTML is full of quoted spans.
+
+**Problem:** AGENT: a quotation with no row is invisible to the gate. `verify.sh` will go green over a misquote. This is the class that shipped “we need some restraint” in essay 5.
+
+Spot-check of the prose quotes against `scripts/find.py` is clean on the fragments I ran (pp. 63–64, 67, 80, 82, 89–90). The process hole is still a ship-blocker.
+
+**Fix:** Rebuild the essay’s quote rows from the HTML before any further edit is called done.
+
+---
+
+### 12. The lede tells the skeptic the verdict before the ledger does
+
+**Location:** lede: “He was wrong about the price and right about almost everything else, including the thing that has actually shaped the field.”
+
+**Problem:** “Almost everything else” is the prophet-vindication sentence. The ledger, on the essay’s own count, is 3 HIT / 1 MISS / 1 RQWA / 1 OPEN at object+problem, and two of those HITs are the ones in (6) and (7). A reader who distrusts “Lem predicted everything” is being asked to accept the conclusion as the hook.
+
+**Fix:** State the tension (cost number vs incentive diagnosis) without “almost everything else.” Let the table argue.
+
+---
+
+### 13. 1964 is a bibliography line, not a state of the art
+
+**Location:** last paragraph of “What Lem said”: Shklovsky 1962, Cocconi–Morrison, Ozma, Byurakan, no exoplanets.
+
+**Problem:** AGENT: the reader must feel 1964. The chapter gives the instrument (Drake’s 27-metre dish, hydrogen-line search, signals no stronger than Earth could then send at ten light-years). That distance — Ozma to GBT/FAST — is the actual baseline for both the cost argument and the p. 80 condition, and it is unused.
+
+**Fix:** Put the Green Bank dish and what it could hear in the said section, so the 2026 receipts have something to be far from.

